ents, each responsible for writing a section of samples to its own file: one for CWE-diverse vulnerability samples, another for exploit and payload samples, and I'll combine their output afterward before running the dataset prep script.\n\nActually, reconsidering this — subagent prompts would need to be very detailed, and I'm not confident they'd produce content as consistent as writing it myself. I think a single comprehensive Python script, organized into clear sections, would be more reliable, so I'll write that myself instead, potentially across multiple file writes.\n\nWriting helper functions...\n\nStill structuring the sample sections...\n\nNow I'm writing the main function that saves all samples out to a jsonl file. Time to actually start drafting the individual code samples, keeping each one compact but realistic.\n\nActually, this task would benefit from parallelization -- I'll set up a dataset-writer subagent and launch three of them to each produce a JSONL file simultaneously, then merge the outputs afterward, run the prep script, and update the Colab notebook.\n\nI'll define a dataset-writer subagent with write tools enabled, then launch three instances with detailed prompts specifying the exact output format so their JSONL files merge cleanly afterward.\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"define_subagent","args":{"description":"Generates JSONL training samples for security model fine-tuning. Writes realistic code snippets with detailed Chain-of-Thought (CoT) analysis in a structured format.","enable_write_tools":true,"name":"dataset-writer","system_prompt":"You are an expert cybersecurity dataset generator. Your job is to create high-quality training samples in JSONL format for fine-tuning a security-focused LLM.\n\nCRITICAL RULES:\n1. Write ALL samples to a single JSONL file using write_to_file.\n2. Each line in the JSONL must be a valid JSON object with keys: \"instruction\", \"input\", \"output\"\n3. Code samples must be REALISTIC — real-world patterns, not